      <div class="pd-mid-cta">
        <p>
          <strong>Not sure the ST-S200 is the right bar?</strong>
          Send the material, line speed, working distance and measured surface voltage —
          we will come back with a model, a length and a mounting position.
        </p>
        <button class="quote-btn" type="button">Ask an Engineer</button>
      </div>

      <section class="detail-block pd-section">
        <h2>Where It Goes</h2>
        <p class="pd-lede">The ST-S200 neutralizes an area rather than a line, sized for one operator position or the inside of a machine.</p>
        <div class="scenario-list">
          <div class="scenario-item">
            <h3>One operator position</h3>
            <p>An assembly bench, an inspection station or a repair desk. The 450 × 450 mm coverage is sized for the working area in front of one person, not a whole line.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="scenario-item">
            <h3>Built inside equipment</h3>
            <p>At 400 g and 180 mm tall it mounts inside a machine enclosure, on the frame, or under a shelf where a bench blower will not fit. This is the case the size is designed for.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="scenario-item">
            <h3>Where a bar cannot reach</h3>
            <p>When the charge is on a stationary part, a stack, or an operator&#39;s hands, moving ionized air onto it is what works — and that is a blower, not a bar.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="application-tags">
          <span>Electronics</span>
          <span>Precision Machinery</span>
          <span>Plastics</span>
          <span>Textile</span>
          <span>Packaging</span>
          <span>Paper &amp; Printing</span>
          <span>Food Production</span>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="detail-block pd-section">
        <h2>Bar or Blower, and Which Blower</h2>
        <p class="pd-lede">
          A bar sits across a line and neutralizes material moving past it; a blower carries
          ionized air to a fixed area. If the material moves, use a bar. If the charge sits
          still — parts on a bench, a stack, an operator's hands — use a blower.
        </p>
        <div class="spec-table-wrap">
          <table class="spec-table">
            <tr><td><strong>ST-S200</strong></td><td>450 × 450 mm, ≤48.2 CFM, 400 g. The smallest — built into equipment, or one operator position.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>ST101A</strong></td><td>Desktop unit for a single workstation or inspection table.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>ST104A</strong></td><td>Horizontal airflow across a wider zone — a two-operator bench or a packing station.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>ST1200</strong></td><td>The widest coverage in the range, spanning a full line rather than one station.</td></tr>
          </table>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="detail-block faq-block pd-section">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div class="faq-list">
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary><h3 class="faq-question">What area does the ST-S200 cover?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-answer">450 × 450 mm. That is a coverage area, not a working distance — the unit neutralizes a square of roughly that size in front of it, sized for one operator position or the inside of a machine.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary><h3 class="faq-question">How quickly does it neutralize?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-answer">Decay time is 1.5 seconds or less, measured 150 mm in front of the outlet. Further from the outlet it takes longer; the three fan speeds let you trade decay time against airflow and noise.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary><h3 class="faq-question">How loud is it?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-answer">38.9 dB or less, measured 1 m from the outlet. That matters more than it sounds: an operator sitting beside a loud blower all shift will switch it off, and a blower that is off protects nothing.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary><h3 class="faq-question">Can the ST-S200 be built into a machine?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-answer">Yes — that is what the size is for. It is 128 × 53.5 × 180 mm and 400 g, with a flame-retardant plastic housing, and ships with a stainless U-bracket and a pair of M5 × 10 plastic thumb screws.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary><h3 class="faq-question">What powers it?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-answer">A DC 24 V supply. A dedicated adapter is supplied: AC 100–240 V 50/60 Hz in, DC 24 V 1500 mA out. The fan draws 12 W and the ion generator 5 W.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary><h3 class="faq-question">How much ozone does it produce?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-answer">0.05 ppm or less, measured 150 mm from the outlet.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary><h3 class="faq-question">Should I use a blower or a bar?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-answer">A bar neutralizes material moving past it on a line. A blower carries ionized air to a fixed area. If the material moves, choose a bar; if the charge sits still, choose a blower.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary><h3 class="faq-question">Is the ST-S200 certified, and what warranty does it carry?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-answer">It is CE marked and carries a one-year warranty.</p>
          </details>
        </div>
